{"id": "x0405", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 11, "onset_chunk": 10, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Tom's ship can travel at 10 miles per hour. He is sailing from 1 to 4 p.m., so that's a total of three hours. If we assume he traveled the same distance both ways, then in three hours at 1 0 miles per hour, he would have gone 30 miles. To get back those 30 miles at 6 miles per hour, it would take him 5 hours.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 22, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0413", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 9, "onset_chunk": 9, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Ted spent a total of $ 7 8. He bought three books at $16 each, which is $ 48, and three pencil s at $6 each, which adds up to $ 18. So, $ 4 8 plus $ 18 equal s $ 7 8 in total.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 21, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0421", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 13, "onset_chunk": 12, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Well, it takes Matthew 3 minutes to dig a small hole for sh rubs. So for 30 small holes, that's going to take him 90 minutes. And then it takes him 10 minutes to dig a large hole for trees. So for 15 large holes, that' s going to take him 1 50 minutes. Add ing those together, he'll need 2 40 minutes in total. Con verting that to hours, it comes out to exactly 4 hours.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 23, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0429", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 16, "onset_chunk": 15, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Greg puts clean sheets on four twin beds and one king-size bed every week. His laundry machine can wash two twin sets at a time and one king set at a time. So, for the twin bed s, since he has four and he can wash two at once, that means he needs to do two load s just for the twin sheet s each week. And then for the king-sized bed, he only needs one load per week. That adds up to three load s of laundry every week. If you multiply that by 52 weeks in a year, Greg does 1 5 6 loads of laundry in a year.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 29, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0437", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 24, "onset_chunk": 23, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So an adult dinos aur will eat 10 pounds of potato salad. A child will eat half as much as an adult, so a child will eat 5 pounds of potato salad. If there are 20 adults and 5 children at the picnic, we can calculate how many pounds of potato salad T ed needs to bring. For the adults: 20 adults times 10 pounds each equal s 20 0 pounds of potato salad. For the children: 5 children times 5 pounds each equals 25 pounds of potato salad. Adding those together, 20 0 pounds plus 2 5 pounds equal s 22 5 pounds of potato salad. Therefore, Ted needs to bring 2 2 5 pounds of potato salad to feed everyone at the picnic.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 44, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0445", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 10, "onset_chunk": 9, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Pierre, Paul and Jo c bought 12 kilogram s of apples. Peter wants a quarter of that, which is 12 divided by 4, equal ing 3 kilograms. Paul wants one third of that, so 12 divided by 3, which is 4 kilo grams. Together, Pe ter and Paul take 3 plus 4, equaling 7 kilogram s. So, starting with 1 2 kilogram s, after they take their share, James will have 1 2 minus 7, leaving him with 5 kilogram s of apples.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 31, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0453", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 27, "onset_chunk": 26, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Let's figure out how far John is from home. First, he drove for 3 hours at 60 miles per hour, so that' s 3 times 6 0, which is 1 80 miles away from home. Then he turns around and tries to get back in 4 hours. For the first 2 hours, he's stuck in traffic, so he doesn't move at all during that time. Next, he drives for half an hour at 3 0 miles per hour, which gets him 15 miles closer. That leaves him with 1 6 5 miles still away after those 2.5 hours. He has 1.5 hours left in his 4- hour trip, but he drives at 80 miles per hour for only part of that time. Wait, let me check that again. Actually, he spends 2 hours stopped, then 0.5 hours driving slowly, so total time used is 2. 5 hours, leaving 1. 5 hours to drive at 8 0 mph. In 1. 5 hours at 80 miles per hour, he covers 12 0 miles. So, he went 1 5 miles back plus 1 20 miles more, which is 1 35 miles total toward", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0461", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 13, "onset_chunk": 12, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "At a restaurant, juice box A is 4 dollars. Ju ice box B is 5 dollars more than juice box A. Juice box C is 7 dollars more than juice box A. How much more is juice box C than juice box B? Well, since juice box C is 7 dollars more than juice box A, and juice box B is only 5 dollars more than juice box A, that means juice box C is 2 dollars more than juice box B. So, juice box C is 2 dollars more expensive than juice box B.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 14, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0469", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 29, "onset_chunk": 29, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, let's figure this out step by step. We have four students: Adam, Martha, Betty, and Tom. Adam has 5 0 points. Bet ty collected 3 0% more than Adam. So we calculate that as 5 0 plus 15, which is 6 5 points for Betty. To m has 3 0 points less than Betty, so that' s 6 5 minus 3 0, which equal s 3 5 points for Tom. Mar tha collected three times more than Tom, so that' s 3 times 35, which is 1 0 5 points for Mar tha.Now, let' s add up all the points. Adam has 50, Be tty has 6 5, Tom has 3 5, and Martha has 1 05. Add ing those together: 5 0 plus 65 is 11 5, plus 35 is 1 50, plus 1 05 is 2 5", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0477", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 12, "onset_chunk": 11, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Jerry counts 6 birds in the bush es. Two thirds of that number is flying overhead, so he sees 4 more birds in the air. And there are three groups of eight birds feeding, which adds up to 24 birds. Add ing them all together—6 plus 4 plus 24— gives him a total count of 3 4 birds.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 24, "carrier_ended": null}
